WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.060
What is the meaning of the white buffalo?&nbsp;
Who is The Monocle? What's Harlan like as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:11.460
an old man? Here are the small details you&nbsp;
missed in the Umbrella Academy season 3.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.840
For those unfamiliar with "The&nbsp;
Umbrella Academy" source material,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:20.520
the comic book series by Gerard Way and&nbsp;
Gabriel Bá that began publishing in 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:25.380
The Hotel Oblivion is a direct reference&nbsp;
to the My Chemical Romance frontman's third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:29.700
volume of the family's story. The moment&nbsp;
in this scene that we're referencing here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:35.600
though, is a tip of the cap to the opening&nbsp;
of Orson Welles' 1941 classic "Citizen Kane."

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:36.600
"Rosebud…"

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:42.240
After seeing Reginald's greater&nbsp;
plan unfold in the season 3 finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.460
it's no surprise he would let out one&nbsp;
particular word in an almost Freudian slip.

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:54.180
"Oblivion…"

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:58.740
Like the phrase "rosebud" in "Citizen&nbsp;
Kane," the remote drop and extreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:02.520
close-up of Reginald's lips puts&nbsp;
tremendous weight on the word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.660
leaving viewers questioning its meaning.&nbsp;
Comic book fans, however, already know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:11.880
The Hotel Oblivion is an impenetrable prison&nbsp;
built by The Monocle to hold super villains.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:16.860
On top of that connection, the finale also&nbsp;
revealed to the Umbrella Academy that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:23.160
Reginald is indeed the green-blooded cyborg alien&nbsp;
known as The Monocle, perhaps making fans even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:28.680
more eager for the next volume of comics, as&nbsp;
well as season 4 of the series based on them.

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:34.980
Eagle-eyed viewers from last season had&nbsp;
been anxiously awaiting the return of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:39.780
young Harlan in season 3. After seeing&nbsp;
he retained Viktor's immense powers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.860
fans speculated that this strange&nbsp;
old man with retro headphones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:49.920
a case of cassette tapes, and a suitcase full of&nbsp;
numbered sandwiches was indeed an older Harlan.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:56.040
The season 2 cliffhanger also teased Harlan's&nbsp;
decimation of Sparrows Jayme and Alphonso,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:02.040
as seen in season 3's third episode. In his hand,&nbsp;
he's levitating a bird which could be a sparrow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:07.680
which foreshadows his explosive outburst&nbsp;
that fries off the Sparrows' faces. Luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:13.020
after that event, viewers received a detailed&nbsp;
backstory depicting the trauma Harlan endured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:17.220
which warped his uncontrollable&nbsp;
abilities. It was subsequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:22.320
explained how Harlan was able to locate the&nbsp;
two superhuman families. His connection to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:27.000
the superpowered heroes resonated in&nbsp;
his mind, almost like sound waves.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:33.360
"The Umbrella Academy" season 3 brought with it&nbsp;
what could potentially be a Marvel connection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.860
at least from the standpoint of visual&nbsp;
effects. If you've never seen the series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:42.840
"Legion" on the FX channel, it follows&nbsp;
an extremely powerful telepathic mutant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:48.180
the eponymous Legion, who struggles with mental&nbsp;
illness. As a result of his mental state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:52.980
it makes it extremely hard for him to&nbsp;
control his powerful abilities. "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:56.880
Umbrella Academy" seems to be evoking&nbsp;
the series, in both look and theme.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:02.700
"Legion" shares some of the same crew members&nbsp;
behind the scenes, such as series composer Jeff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:08.340
Russo and director of photography Craig&nbsp;
Wrobleski. This likely explains why the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:13.860
explosive outburst from Harlan at the beginning&nbsp;
of Episode 4 is so similar to David's kitchen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:19.320
utensil tornado. They even look and sound&nbsp;
similar, with cool blue tones and chaotic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:25.260
whirlwinds of dangerous objects. Both explosions&nbsp;
also come from a character's deep trauma.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:31.380
So even if it wasn't an intentional callback&nbsp;
to that other trippy superhero tale, the style&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:36.120
and themes definitely carry over thanks to the&nbsp;
talent designing and implementing the effects.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:41.340
The most familiar face in the Sparrow&nbsp;
Academy worked his way up to a brand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:46.320
new ranking in season 3. The previous&nbsp;
Number Six in the family relentlessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:51.240
devoted himself to becoming one of the&nbsp;
strongest members of The Sparrows. Ben's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:55.620
horrific powers allow him to unleash&nbsp;
the fearsome tentacled beast within,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:59.760
and it seems his fierce dedication has&nbsp;
earned him an upgrade to Number Two.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:03.780
There's already enough bad blood&nbsp;
between The Umbrella Academy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:08.340
Sparrows, because of the first team's&nbsp;
arrival in the new reality. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:13.620
when Ben discovers he died in their original&nbsp;
timeline, he's far less trustworthy of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:19.200
rambunctious new siblings. After the first&nbsp;
episode, with Marcus taken by the kugelblitz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.820
Ben declares himself the new Number One,&nbsp;
but in Episode three his siblings make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:27.840
passing remark that tips off the audience&nbsp;
that Ben was the original Number One.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.820
"You had your chance once. Didn't&nbsp;
turn out too well now, did it?"

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:38.580
So, what did he do to get taken down a peg? Was&nbsp;
this Reginald's twisted way of motivating him?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:42.300
Whatever the reason, his resentment&nbsp;
is suddenly far more understandable.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:47.880
Not surprisingly, there are more than a few&nbsp;
references to "The Umbrella Academy" source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:53.820
material, one being a new look for fan-favorite&nbsp;
Rumor. After Allison unsuccessfully tries to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:58.860
"rumor" herself into happiness, she grabs&nbsp;
a shard of broken mirror and cuts her hair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:03.660
short. The significance of this transformation&nbsp;
matches her signature style in the comics.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:08.940
Emmy Raver-Lampman showcases a deeply&nbsp;
moving performance as Allison in season 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:13.860
gutted to learn her daughter Claire doesn't&nbsp;
exist in this timeline. This sends Allison&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:18.900
into a downward spiral, where she even rumors&nbsp;
Luther into wanting to stay with her because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:24.060
a desperate desire for any sense of something&nbsp;
familiar. She also argues with Viktor about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:28.500
Harlan's fate, and eventually kills the&nbsp;
old man for what he did to The Sparrows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:34.080
This was Allison's darkest season yet, and it&nbsp;
closely resembles her comic book counterpart.

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:40.860
The Hotel Oblivion finally makes its frightening&nbsp;
debut through a passageway behind a wall-mounted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:46.500
pachinko machine located in the Buffalo Suite&nbsp;
of The Hotel Obsidian. The hotel is similar to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:51.480
The Overlook from "The Shining," albeit bolstered&nbsp;
with interesting additions like a sushi carousel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:57.240
murderous samurai guardians, and a built-in,&nbsp;
universe-changing machine. There's also an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:02.580
abundance of gross cockroaches, which Lila&nbsp;
happily squashes…another comic book reference.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:08.220
The biggest note of importance, with the&nbsp;
arrival in Oblivion, is the reveal of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:14.100
ambitious scheme Reginald cooks up. It adds&nbsp;
a necessary layer of suspicion, which pays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:19.860
off when Allison kills him. The green blood and&nbsp;
sparking metallic skeleton is an extraordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:24.960
visual queue, one that reveals to everyone&nbsp;
that Reginald was not who he seemed to be.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:30.420
It also illustrates the brilliance and dedication&nbsp;
of Reginald, who purposefully built the hotel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:35.400
around a portal he discovered. Once the audience&nbsp;
discovers he is the founder of the hotel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:41.040
everything begins coming together, as the maniacal&nbsp;
genius of The Monocle is revealed at last.

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:44.880
Potentially the deepest mystery of the season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:49.380
this Easter egg is quite intriguing.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, even the comics can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:53.880
help viewers much here, but it does reveal&nbsp;
some information about the elusive Jennifer.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:59.160
In Episode 7, after a heated argument&nbsp;
with Allison, Viktor runs to his old room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:03.600
which is now Ben's in the new timeline. On&nbsp;
his table, there is a drawing of a beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.400
woman with the name Jennifer scrawled on the&nbsp;
bottom. In the comics, The Jennifer Incident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:14.040
is a newspaper clipping posted in Spaceboy's&nbsp;
base on the moon. The accompanying photo is of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:18.720
Luther with his hand blocking the camera lens.&nbsp;
There are various other clippings visible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:24.120
including one with the headline "The Horror&nbsp;
– Dead!" In the comics, Ben is referred to as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:27.900
The Horror, and with this scene from the&nbsp;
series, we can assume that "The Jennifer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:33.120
Incident," and maybe even Jennifer herself,&nbsp;
caused Ben's death in the original timeline.

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:40.320
The opening scene from the season 1 finale&nbsp;
revealed Abigail Hargreeves, Reginald's love from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:45.900
"long ago." It turns out that Luther was guarding&nbsp;
her cryogenically frozen corpse on the moon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:51.420
though he's unaware of that fact. This adds a&nbsp;
much deeper purpose to Luther's time on the moon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:57.780
while also exposing how selfish Reginald is&nbsp;
to sacrifice his son's sanity and happiness.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:03.420
Revisiting the flashback from season 1, there's&nbsp;
another detail far more significant after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:08.220
events of the new season. The glowing orange&nbsp;
particles that Reginald released into the air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:12.780
as the rockets were taking off, are&nbsp;
the source of power for all the gifted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:17.340
children in the series. It's also fuel&nbsp;
for the otherworldly machine that averted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:21.420
the apocalypse for the third time. Could&nbsp;
Abigail be where the particles came from?

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:25.980
"I can't leave you. There must be another way."

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:32.700
Abigail is Reginald's singular purpose for&nbsp;
every merciless action he has taken thus far.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:38.820
This explains his triumphant expression, in the&nbsp;
season 3 finale, when they're finally reunited.

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:43.800
After the kugelblitz annihilates&nbsp;
everything in sight, "The Umbrella&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:48.900
Academy" makes a dramatic turn, and instead of&nbsp;
the show's usual fade to black in the credits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:54.780
it fades to a blinding white. This stylistic&nbsp;
choice emphasizes the gravity of the situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:57.420
the undeniable power of the kugelblitz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:02.880
and the beautiful sacrifice Klaus makes so he&nbsp;
would still be around to help his siblings.

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:08.220
The fade to white could also symbolize The White&nbsp;
Buffalo that Klaus killed himself on. The White&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:12.780
Buffalo has a rich and deep Native American&nbsp;
meaning that represents hope and abundance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.780 --> 00:09:18.420
This is a surprisingly comforting note to end&nbsp;
on after the kugelblitz devours everything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:24.060
but after The Umbrella Academy saved the world&nbsp;
twice, it's not wise to doubt them at this point.

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:29.760
Russo's angelic score pauses for a brief&nbsp;
moment before bursting into the rousing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:32.520
"Umbrella Academy" theme. This music change reads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:36.360
as a fitting call to action for our&nbsp;
heroes to save the world, yet again.

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:42.540
If there is a fourth season of "The Umbrella&nbsp;
Academy," Ben will surely play a big part in it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:48.120
The cryptic after-credits scene shows Ben sitting&nbsp;
alone on a subway train reading. While you can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:52.800
exactly make out the book he's holding, you can&nbsp;
overhear the conductor say where Ben is arriving.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:56.700
Ben is riding to the Yeouido&nbsp;
train station in South Korea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.700 --> 00:10:01.800
but what is he doing there? Could this be related&nbsp;
to The Jennifer Incident? One thing is for sure:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:06.600
saving the world didn't bring Ben any closer&nbsp;
to his siblings if he's traveling all the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:10.800
to South Korea by himself. His anger is&nbsp;
certainly justifiable, but if he stays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:15.600
alone and allows it to consume him, he could&nbsp;
suffer a fate even worse than his original self.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:18.600
It's also been speculated that this Ben is in fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:22.920
a doppelganger of the Sparrow version&nbsp;
of the character. If that's the case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:26.760
what does it mean about potential doubles&nbsp;
for the other members of the family?

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:33.120
The plaque that commemorates the donation&nbsp;
of Obsidian Memorial Park feels more like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:37.500
a slight against the saviors of space and&nbsp;
time rather than a heartwarming tribute.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:42.180
But knowing Reginald, and after seeing his&nbsp;
egomania run rampant and take over the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:45.480
it probably was meant to be&nbsp;
a middle finger to his kids.

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:50.160
The icing on the cake of Reginald's&nbsp;
less-than-sentimental donation is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:54.240
it was donated on their birthday,&nbsp;
right down to the year. Reginald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:59.340
was willing to let all seven of his gifted&nbsp;
children die to be reunited with Abigail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:04.080
It's easy to believe he's not above a&nbsp;
smug "gracious donation" such as this.

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:05.760
"As—le."

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:09.360
With their powers gone and The&nbsp;
Monocle's true nature revealed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:14.640
what's next for this dysfunctional bunch of&nbsp;
former superheroes? Hopefully, a fourth season.

